CONF6809 -- an OS-9/6809 conformance suite

What this is: a small set of standalone tests, each checking one
documented Microware OS-9 behaviour against what this system actually
does. Every test prints exactly one RESULT line (PASS, FAIL, SKIP or
ERROR); see DOCS/claims for the manual citation behind each one. This
is not a regression suite for any particular emulator -- it exists to
compare real hardware against the documentation.

How to run it, from the OS-9 shell, after mounting this disk as /x0:

    chd /x0
    load procs
    load runb
    chx /x0/CMDS
    runall

The "load" lines matter and are not cosmetic: this account has no PATH
set, so once "chx /x0/CMDS" points command lookup at this disk's
own CMDS/, "procs" (runall's own first real step) and the packed BASIC09
"tally" (which needs RunB to run it) both become unfindable unless they
are already memory-resident first. "load" makes each one resident before
CHX moves away from wherever they normally live, so both keep working
afterward regardless of CHX. Confirmed live: skipping either "load" line
reproduces "Error #216 - Path Name Not Found" partway through the run --
see task-4-report.md.

What it writes: everything lands under /x0 -- RESULTS/report (a RUN
line naming the run, the process list from "procs" so the report
records who ran it, then one RESULT line per test) and whatever a
test's own SCRATCH/ working files were. Nothing outside this disk is
ever touched; no test writes to /dd. This was checked directly, not
assumed: a full run, a rebuild, and a deliberate SKIP were all driven
in one continuous session while /dd's own directory listing was
snapshotted before and after, and it came back byte-for-byte
unchanged.

A note on the record-locking tests, because they behave unlike the
others: three of them start a second process of their own and wait for
it. They are the only way to check a mechanism that exists purely to
coordinate concurrent access, so a run now takes a minute or two rather
than seconds, most of it apparently idle, and "procs" during a run will
show helper processes alongside the test. That is the test working, not
a hang. Each of them also refuses to report a verdict it cannot stand
behind: if its helper did not finish, or the situation it needed could
not be set up, it reports ERROR rather than guessing at FAIL. A slow
system is a reason to wait longer, never a reason to read a number.

What a RESULT line means: PASS is the documented behaviour matching
what this system actually did; FAIL is a real disagreement -- the
system did something the cited manual passage says it should not.
SKIP means the test could not exercise its claim at all on this run,
most often because it needs an operator who is not this disk's
superuser, and it says so in its own message rather than guessing;
SKIP is not a failure and does not count against this system. ERROR
means the test itself could not establish the situation it needed to
check (its own setup step failed) and is also not the same thing as
FAIL. If a shell run produces "WHAT?" from runall or runone
themselves (not from rebuild -- see below), that is not a verdict at
all: it means this shell did not recognise a command in the
procedure, most likely because a "load" step was skipped or ran on
the wrong disk.

Ownership: every file this disk ships is owned by ID 1.7 ($0107 when
read as one flat 16-bit value -- 68k-style group.user display, but
6809's own F$ID and this suite's own guard tests read it as the
single flat number 263, same as any other non-zero ID). This is the
disk-building account, not a superuser or a group-privileged
identity by either OS-9's own reading: only user ID 0 is superuser
here (see DOCS/claims.md's t05 citation). So none of these files run
with any elevated privilege on this system or yours; what makes them
usable to another account at all is the public permission bits set
on each one (readable, and for CMDS/ and the top-level procedures,
executable) -- ordinary public-permission access, the same as any
file you did not create yourself.

runone reruns the same set of shipped tests as runall, without the
"procs" header, appending to the same report -- useful for rechecking
after a partial run. runall, runone and rebuild always invoke the same
set of tests; the build verifies this.

tally reads RESULTS/report and prints:

    CONF6809 totals: PASS=n FAIL=n SKIP=n ERROR=n

It counts every RESULT line in the file regardless of which run wrote
it, so run it right after the run you care about if you want that
run's own totals.

Redirection note, since the Unix habit is wrong here: on this shell
">>" redirects standard ERROR, not append. ">" fails if the target
already exists. This suite's own procedures use ">+" (append to an
existing file, or create it) for every RESULT-line write, and that
form was confirmed live before it was relied on -- both a two-line
append-in-order check and the negative (">>" puts nothing on the
stdout path) -- see task-4-report.md.

Rebuilding: CMDS/ ships prebuilt so the suite runs with nothing but a
shell. SRC/ carries the exact sources CMDS/ was built from. Running
"rebuild" (after the setup above -- it needs "procs"/RunB resident
same as any other run) reassembles SRC/*.a with this system's own asm
and repacks SRC/*.bas with its own basic09, writing the results into
REBUILT/ -- never into CMDS/ -- then runs the rebuilt suite from
there. CMDS/ is never modified, by any path: a recipient whose asm or
basic09 is present but produces something incompatible -- the case
most worth hearing about -- still ends up with a working prebuilt
CMDS/ *and* whatever REBUILT/ turned out to be, never neither. If this
system has no asm or basic09 at /DD/CMDS, rebuild prints one line
saying so and writes nothing to REBUILT/.

Each run, prebuilt or rebuilt, starts by writing a boundary line --
"RUN prebuilt" or "RUN rebuilt" -- into RESULTS/report before anything
else. The report is append-only, so this is the only thing that lets
a later reader split a report containing both runs back into two.

Sending results back: please send the whole of RESULTS/report, both
runs included if you ran rebuild (or just the whole disk image), not
a hand-trimmed excerpt -- "list RESULTS/report" from the OS-9 shell
will show it on screen if you have no easier way to copy it off. The
project lives at https://github.com/peacedudes/os9exec -- open an
issue there and attach or paste the report, noting what real or
emulated 6809 hardware and OS-9 version you ran it on. A FAIL is the
most useful thing you can report: this suite exists to find exactly
that kind of disagreement between the documentation and a real
system, on hardware and OS-9 builds we may not have access to
ourselves.

"rebuild" needs a shell with IF/ELSE/ENDIF (Shell+ or equivalent) to
guard against a missing asm or basic09; runall and runone deliberately
use no conditionals at all, so the core suite runs on the plainest
shell that can read a procedure file. If your shell does not recognise
a keyword, it prints "WHAT?" instead of running that line -- if you
see that from "rebuild", it means your shell lacks IF/ELSE/ENDIF, not
that the disk is broken; runall and runone should never produce it.
